\f1\fs22 Chilo wasn\'92t the seventh among the seven sages. He simply wasn\'92t! Either they were a band of equals, or Chilo was among the best of them. Certainly among the top five, and certainly more indispensable than Solo.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Or, so thought Chilo. It was obvious he was right about so many things. While he believed in the facilitation of worthy prophecies, he often differed on what constituted 
\f2\i worthy
\f1\i0 . Still, it had been a lean few generations, and Chilo agreed Kleoboulos\'92 side project was the worthiest prophecy on offer. It was, axiomatically, also the unworthiest.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>And so Chilo went about his sagery determined to act as a foil to Kleoboulos\'92 enigmatic approach. Fuck aiming for the smallest intervention. Fuck systems theory. And yes, sometimes, fuck rational enlightenment. For example, was their own universe so bad? Was it as bum-fucked aa Kleoboulos claimed? Chilo thought not. After all, the scourge of democracy had never flourished anywhere in their universe, and, while it excelled in superstition, misinformation, conspiracy theories, greed, economic rationalism, corruption and, fundamentally, all other the kinds of stupidity, had it not that same seething blunt amalgam of cruelty which produced an unparalleled social order, where the strong prospered and the weak were left in the wilderness to wither? It was beautiful in its militaristic authoritarianism.\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sure, there had been some heinous actors throughout history, 
\f1\i0 Chilo thought.
\f2\i  Dewey Dunning-Kruger (one-time President of the Squabbling States of America), Jamison Hardy, Linus Grayling (founder of Grayling Corporation), and, more recently, Eloise Shakti
\f1\i0 , 
\f2\i Look at it this way: sure, Grayling Corporation reinvented slavery when they sold ELVIS clones, but look how it turned out: we\'92ve got more Elvises than any other universe in the history of the multiverse.
\f1\i0  Like most authoritarian social order characters in the brief history of time-wasting, Chilo could savour any flavour of evil, as long as it was in the name of maintaining stability.\
\pard\pardeftab360\sl360\slmult1\partightenfactor0

